WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:01.334 line:92%
-Hey, David. How are you?

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.569 line:92%
-I'm good. How are you, Seth

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:05.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm doing very well.
Congrats on your new show.

00:00:05.305 --> 00:00:07.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm very jealous.
I feel like for a long time,

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:11.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
my dream was to shoot a comedy
in England,

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:14.047 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's exactly
what you got to do.

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:16.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. I feel really,
really blessed, actually.

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:19.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a lot of fun
to work in -- in the U.K.

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.522
-And you -- Did you know
the creator of this show?

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
How did you get involved
in the first place?

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:27.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, he's a buddy of mine,
Nick Mohammed.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:33.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, he and this othe
writer and actor, Julia Davis,

00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:34.968 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
had created a show

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:39.139
that they were thinking abou
adding an American character to.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.073 line:92%
This was a couple years ago.

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:45.111
So I went out to London to d
about a week of improv with them

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:48.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
and just kind of
find that character

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:50.383 line:92%
and find who this guy would be

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:54.888
We discovered a really fun
dynamic between Nick and I

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:58.958 size:84% position:42%
of kind of a difference
in status and power.

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:02.228 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I guess Nick
really loved that dynamic.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we had such a good tim
working together

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:07.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
that about a year later,
he just e-mailed me this idea.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I just thought it was --
I was just so tickled

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the idea of a workplace
comedy

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:17.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
set in the high-stakes world
of national security

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:19.712 line:92%
and cyber terrorism that I -

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:24.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
and this guy who's such
a pompous American blowhard.

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:27.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just thought it was a really
fun opportunity, so...

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:30.890
-It does seem like a character
that you sink your teeth into.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.928
Did you know from the earl
creation of the character

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:37.530
or the fact that it was this
workplace intelligence comed

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you wanted to be the bras
sort of blowhardy

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.568 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
American character?

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:43.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That's kind of who he was
from the offset.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.739
And we worked on it then
together for about a year,

00:01:46.739 --> 00:01:50.610
year and a half, trying to
find -- trying to find this guy,

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:56.149
who, to me, is inspired from
a lot of men in power in our -

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:58.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-In our country

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:00.820 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
and probably his,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:05.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
this, you know --
This mostly ignorant narcissis

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:13.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
and casually -- casually sexis
and racist and homophobic

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:17.170 align:start size:94% position:6%
and -- and very patriotic,
mostly because

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
he's never been out of
the United States in his life.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Chuckling ] Right.
Were you --

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:26.946
You know, obviously, we both
have backgrounds as improvisers,

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:28.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I haven't done it
for so long.

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:29.716 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think I would be
a little terrified

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
if somebody said, "Hey,
let's go -- Let's improvise.

00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:34.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
Did you -- Was it all --
Did it all come back to you,

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:36.022 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
or was it scary?

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:40.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was -- It wasn't scary
but I -- You know, I --

00:02:40.527 --> 00:02:45.532
I know your background of improv
and Boom Chicago and everything.

00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:48.635 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know,
I learned early on

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:51.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
from when I was in
an improv group with Colbert

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:54.641
that I --
He just outclassed me, you know?

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:58.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I-I kind of approach i
when I'm in --

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:00.813 size:88% position:44%
I think it's a little --
I approach it more like

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:01.781 size:81% position:41% line:92%
an actor in character.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:04.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
As long as I've got
a really solid character

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:08.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
and kind of an idea
of what the scene's about,

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.523 line:92%
I feel permission to improv.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.659 size:75% position:38% line:92%
And Nick is so good.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's as good as you
and Colbert at it.

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:19.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I feel like he carried me
pretty much, so...

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:22.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You -- So, you brought up
Colbert. Now I need to say it.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:27.740 align:start size:94% position:6%
Colbert, you, myself, our othe
guest tonight, Robin Thede

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
all graduates of
Northwestern University.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:31.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I know we've talked
about it before.

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:32.645 line:92%
You spoke at my graduation

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:35.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I know that's like --
There's a good and bad to that

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:39.118
'cause, one, I feel like
I'm -- I'm age-shaming you

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:40.386 size:75% position:38%
But two, you gave...
-You are. You are.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:43.289
Thank you for that. Thanks
-...the best commencement speech

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:45.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've ever -- I mean,
I haven't heard that many,

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:48.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it was outstanding.
-Well, thank you.

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:50.797
-And then -- So right out
of college, did you --

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:53.299
I know Lookingglass was your
theater company in Chicago

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:56.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that right out of colleg
that you started that?

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:58.504 size:81% position:41%
-Yeah, we started upon
graduation, actually,

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:01.507
from Northwestern, maybe because
we just didn't want --

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:04.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were too scared
to enter the real world.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:07.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, yeah, we --
We really were hungry

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.916 align:start size:94% position:6%
and determined
to start our own company.

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:12.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
We had been directing
each other in --

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:15.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
throughout, you know,
our years at Northwestern.

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:17.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then I started
to bounce back

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:21.094 align:start size:94% position:6%
between auditioning
for TV and film in L.A.

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.528 line:92%
and commuting to Chicago.

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:25.031 size:88% position:44%
So I'd go back and forth
to do theater in Chicago

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:28.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
with my company and try to -
try to climb

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:32.639
the, you know, the Hollywood
you know, rungs, as it were,

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:38.011
the ladder, to try to eke out
career in TV and film, as well

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:41.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's funny. You know,
obviously, you know, I think

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:44.150
like everybody in that cast, you
came to prominence on "Friends."

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:47.053 align:start size:94% position:6%
I did not realize that you wer
on another iconic show.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.021 line:92%
I did not realize till today

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:50.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you were on
"The Wonder Years."

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:51.291 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:55.395
-And based on this photo, a very
different character than Ross.

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:59.365
-I was playing this -- this kind
of hippie -- not hippie,

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:04.904 align:start size:94% position:6%
but more like a wandering
construction kind of guy and -

00:05:04.904 --> 00:05:08.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
who fell in love
with Olivia Dabo's character

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:11.177 size:78% position:39% line:92%
you know, the sister.

00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:15.048 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that was my first
introduction to TV, really

00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:17.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, that was
my first real break.

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:20.119 line:92%
And I can tell you, the first --

00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:24.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
Actually, my first real scen
on-camera --

00:05:24.524 --> 00:05:25.858 line:92%
You know, I come from theater,

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:27.994 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I had no idea
what I was doing, right?

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:31.364
I just thought, "Okay, we al
rehearse this big dinner scene."

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:34.100 align:start size:94% position:6%
Actually, about five of us
sat at the table.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:37.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we rehearsed,
and I felt kind of nervous

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:40.707
I'm stepping into this big cast,
this huge show.

00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:43.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they shoot --
They shoot Fred first.

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:46.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
They shoot him 'cause he had t
go to school after his --

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:48.681 line:92%
-[ Laughs ] Right, of course

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:50.416 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-So, they shot him out.

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:53.986
And then they shoot
everyone else out, and I'm last.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.621 line:92%
And when I'm finally on-camera

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:57.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm acting across
a piece of tape

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:00.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they put
next to the camera box

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:04.764
and some older script supervisor
kind of reading the lines.

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:07.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm like, "Oh, my God.
This is my intro --

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:09.001 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm just acting
with a piece of tape."

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:12.905
Anyway, it was -- It was baptism
by fire, I guess, but, you know.

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:14.640 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think the other weird thing
that happens

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the first time
you're on television --

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:20.513 size:84% position:42%
My first time, I was --
had a very small role

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:22.448 line:92%
in an episode of "Spin City.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.318 align:start size:94% position:6%
People at home, they see you
and they assume,

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:26.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
since you've been
on network television,

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:29.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you have now made it,
and you have nothing but money

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:32.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the reality is, like,
as soon as I did that episode,

00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:33.459 line:92%
I went back to my normal life.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the same happened
for you, right?

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:40.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
-For sure. I mean, I was
waiting tables for seven years

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:43.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
during all these little gigs
you know?

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:44.537 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're right.
I mean, people think,

00:06:44.537 --> 00:06:45.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Wow, you're on TV.
You made it!"

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:49.542 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, I mean, the first tim
I actually saw myself

00:06:49.542 --> 00:06:51.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
on television,
on "The Wonder Years,"

00:06:51.711 --> 00:06:54.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was waiting tables --
I was on my shift.

00:06:54.947 --> 00:06:58.885
And one of my buddies,
another waiter, you know,

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:02.488
pointed out to the televisio
that we had over the bar -

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:04.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the bar area
of the restaurant.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
He said, "Schwimmer,
you're on TV."

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:09.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, and I turn,
and I looked. I'm like, "Yeah.

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:09.996 line:92%
Then I turn back to my table

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:13.299
I was like, "Do you want
blue cheese or thousand island?"

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:14.967 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:17.503 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
It's very humbling.

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:18.805 line:92%
It's -- That's a good story.

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:22.241
I hope that everybody who'
trying to get into this business

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:24.510 align:start size:94% position:6%
can hear that
and be a little inspired

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.579 line:92%
by how it ultimately turned out.

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:31.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, it also -- I think
it teaches you so much respect

00:07:31.651 --> 00:07:33.653 align:start size:94% position:6%
for anyone
in the service industry,

00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:34.454 line:92%
for the rest of your life.

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:36.189 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, if you've ever
waited tables,

00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:39.492 align:start size:94% position:6%
as I did for seven years,
first of all,

00:07:39.492 --> 00:07:42.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
you tip really well
for the rest of your life...

00:07:42.829 --> 00:07:44.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Absolutely.
-...because you just know

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:48.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
how much -- how much
people rely on gratuity.

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:51.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's the only way
I paid my bills,

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:53.306 line:92%
you know, for all those years.

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:55.374 size:84% position:42%
-I also -- I don't know
if you feel this way,

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:57.109 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think there's
this projection

00:07:57.109 --> 00:07:59.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
of how stressful
show business can be.

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:00.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not
without its stresses,

00:08:00.947 --> 00:08:03.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the only stress dreams
I ever have

00:08:03.382 --> 00:08:05.551 line:92%
are ones about being a waiter.

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:06.486 size:81% position:41% line:92%
Like, that is still --

00:08:06.486 --> 00:08:09.188 size:84% position:42%
That, like, table seven
needs their burrito.

00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:10.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
That will wake me up
in the middle of the night

00:08:10.957 --> 00:08:12.959
more than anything
that ever happened in my career.

00:08:12.959 --> 00:08:14.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, I hope,
one, that we can --

00:08:14.193 --> 00:08:16.229 size:81% position:41%
all of us can get back
to restaurants soon.

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:17.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, two,
that we all can remember

00:08:17.897 --> 00:08:19.332 line:92%
to over-tip our wait staff

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:21.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, yes.
-And congrats on the show.

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:22.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's always so nice
talking to you, David.

00:08:22.802 --> 00:08:25.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thanks, man. Thank, Seth.
I appreciate it.

